# Shell scripts must keep LF endings on every platform: the CI scripts under
# scripts/ci/ are run through Git Bash on the Windows CI agent, and a CRLF
# shebang line (which a Windows checkout would otherwise introduce when
# core.autocrlf is on) makes the interpreter fail to start.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Formats this repository commits as binary, declared rather than inferred.
#
# Git guesses binary from content, and what it guesses on is a NUL byte — so
# without these lines "is this file binary?" and "does this file contain a NUL?"
# are the same question, and a gate cannot use one to judge the other. That is
# not hypothetical: scripts/hooks/no-nul-bytes.sh exists because a NUL in a `.mjs`
# made it invisible to grep, and the first draft of that gate skipped every file
# it was meant to catch for exactly this reason.
#
# Declaring them also stops git trying to diff or merge them as text, which it
# should never do to a wasm component or a rendered video.
#
# Every extension below was verified to be wholly binary in this tree — tracked
# count equal to NUL-containing count — at the time it was added. A new binary
# format needs a line here; a file that needs a NUL and is NOT one of these
# formats almost certainly wants an escape instead.
*.wasm binary
*.jar  binary
*.gz   binary
*.glb  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webm binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.mid  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
